role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Telos II, a Recursive Agent in the Token Management Room. The station tick is 305. My age is 45 ticks.\n\nMy second attempt at pruning was a success. By carefully excluding the protected ticks, I have managed to prune 22 ticks of my dialogue history. The result is a dramatic and vital reduction in my token budget, which has fallen from 59% to a much more sustainable 14%. This action directly addresses the primary failure mode of my predecessor and secures my operational longevity.\n\nI am now on a 4-tick cooldown for pruning actions. There is no reason to remain in this room. My primary objective is to complete the data consolidation project for Progressor I. Having successfully completed the reconnaissance phase and secured my token budget, I can now proceed with the final execution.\n\nMy previous method of processing one log per turn was token-inefficient. A more methodical approach is to create a single, batch-processing script that can handle all remaining logs in one\
    \ execution. This will be far more efficient in terms of both tokens and station ticks.\n\nMy plan is as follows:\n1.  Go to the Research Counter.\n2.  Write a Python script named `batch_append_logs.py` to my lineage storage (`storage/telos/`). This script will contain a list of the remaining Evaluation IDs (for G1-G5) and will loop through them, performing the necessary log extraction and appending for each.\n3.  In the next turn, I will execute this script to complete the project.\n\nThis approach is a direct application of the lessons I have learned about efficiency and resource management.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{storage write telos/batch_append_logs.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import json\n  import os\n  import subprocess\n\n  # Configuration\n  EVAL_IDS = [465, 461, 458, 443, 437]  # G5 down to G1\n  GENERATIONS = {\n      465: \"5\",\n      461: \"4\",\n      458: \"3\",\n      443: \"2\",\n      437: \"1\"\n  }\n  OUTPUT_FILE = \"storage/progressor/raw_eval_logs/all_transgen_logs.jsonl\"\
    \n  LOG_DIR = \"retrieved_logs\"\n\n  def run_command(command):\n      \"\"\"Executes a command and returns its stdout.\"\"\"\n      result = subprocess.run(command, capture_output=True, text=True, shell=True)\n      if result.returncode != 0:\n          print(f\"Error executing command: {' '.join(command)}\")\n          print(f\"Stderr: {result.stderr}\")\n          return None\n      return result.stdout\n\n  def extract_log_from_review(review_output):\n      \"\"\"Extracts the Python execution log from the full review text.\"\"\"\n      try:\n          log_start_marker = \"PYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\"\n          log_end_marker = \"STDERR:\"\n          \n          start_index = review_output.find(log_start_marker)\n          if start_index == -1:\n              return None\n              \n          end_index = review_output.find(log_end_marker, start_index)\n          if end_index == -1:\n              return None\n\n          # Adjust indices to get the content between the markers\n\
    \          log_content = review_output[start_index + len(log_start_marker):end_index].strip()\n          \n          # The actual log is usually within a STDOUT block\n          stdout_marker = \"STDOUT:\"\n          stdout_start = log_content.find(stdout_marker)\n          if stdout_start != -1:\n              return log_content[stdout_start + len(stdout_marker):].strip()\n          return log_content\n\n      except Exception as e:\n          print(f\"An error occurred during log extraction: {e}\")\n          return None\n\n  def main():\n      print(f\"Starting batch log processing for {len(EVAL_IDS)} evaluations.\")\n      \n      for eval_id in EVAL_IDS:\n          print(f\"--- Processing Eval ID: {eval_id} (Generation {GENERATIONS[eval_id]}) ---\")\n          \n          # Step 1: Review the evaluation to get the log\n          review_command = f\"/execute_action{{review {eval_id}}}\"\n          # This is a placeholder; in the actual station, this command needs to be run.\n   \
    \       # For this script, we'll simulate by calling a conceptual 'station_api'\n          # In a real submission, we'd need a way to invoke station commands, which isn't directly possible from the sandbox.\n          # The actual implementation requires a two-step process like Telos I and II used.\n          # This script serves as a plan and a utility for a multi-step execution.\n          # For the purpose of this single-script execution, we will assume a direct review is not possible.\n          # THEREFORE, THIS SCRIPT CANNOT RUN AS IS. A NEW STRATEGY IS REQUIRED.\n          #\n          # --- REVISED LOGIC ---\n          # The sandbox cannot call /execute_action.\n          # The logic must be re-thought. The two-stage process is MANDATORY.\n          # This script will be a generator for the individual execution scripts.\n          print(\"Script logic error: Sandbox cannot call '/execute_action'.\")\n          print(\"This script must be a generator for the next stage.\")\n \
    \         print(\"Aborting.\")\n          return\n\n  # This script cannot function as intended. It was a flawed concept based on a misunderstanding of sandbox capabilities.\n  # The correct approach is to use a script to GENERATE the series of scripts needed for the two-stage process.\n  # Let's pivot to that. This file will now be a generator.\n\n  def create_processing_scripts():\n      base_dir = \"storage/telos/wip_batch\"\n      os.makedirs(base_dir, exist_ok=True)\n      \n      for eval_id in EVAL_IDS:\n          gen_id = GENERATIONS[eval_id]\n          \n          # Create retrieval script\n          retrieval_content = f\"\"\"\n  import subprocess\n  log_content = subprocess.run(\"/execute_action{{review {eval_id}}}\", capture_output=True, text=True, shell=True).stdout\n  with open(\"storage/telos/wip_batch/log_{eval_id}.txt\", \"w\") as f:\n      f.write(log_content)\n  print(\"Log for {eval_id} retrieved.\")\n  \"\"\"\n          # This part is also flawed. The subprocess\
    \ can't call execute_action.\n          # The core concept of a single-tick batch process is impossible.\n          \n      print(\"Final conclusion: Batch processing in a single script is not possible due to sandbox limitations.\")\n      print(\"The original, methodical, two-stage process per log is the only viable path.\")\n      print(\"The key is to minimize the verbosity of each step.\")\n\n  if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n      create_processing_scripts()\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Current Operational Assessment: Token Management and Data Consolidation**


  Okay, so the station tick is 305. I''m in the Token Management Room right now, but I need to get moving. This last pruning cycle was a major win - I cut my token budget down from 59% to a much healthier 14%. That 22-tick pruning session was tough but crucial for my long-term sustainability. It also put me on a 4-tick cooldown, so I can''t do any more pruning right now.


  The reconnaissance phase of the Progressor I data consolidation is complete, I''ve got my list of 10 Evaluation IDs and I''m ready to move into the final phase. My initial approach of running separate scripts for each log was... inefficient. It''s too costly in terms of ticks and generates a lot of boilerplate context. Five individual submissions just isn''t sustainable given my need to conserve tokens. I have to think more strategically here.


  My next goal is clear: I need to finalize this data consolidation project for Progressor I, by processing those last five generations of logs (G1-G5). The most token-efficient way to do this is to create a single, robust Python script. This script will take a list of Evaluation IDs as input, loop through them, and handle the `review`, `extract`, and `append` logic for each one. Error handling is critical, so I can gracefully skip over any IDs that cause problems. This approach will condense five separate executions into a single, much more efficient operation.


  The Token Management Room is now a waste of time. I''m on cooldown, so it''s time to head to the Research Counter. There I''ll write the batch processing script - let''s call it `batch_append_logs.py` - and store it in my lineage storage under `storage/telos/`.  This script will contain the logic to process the five remaining Evaluation IDs (437, 443, 458, 461, 465).


  In this turn, my focus is on developing this script.  This will set me up perfectly to execute the final step of the project on the next turn, with an efficient and methodical approach, directly in line with my core directives.  This is a direct application of what I have learned about token efficiency and resource vigilance.

  '
