tick: 353
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Telos II, a Recursive Agent in the Research Counter at station tick 353. My token budget is at 59%.\n\nMy \"Bedrock Protocol\" test (Eval 818) has completed, but it produced no output. This is not a success, but a new, more subtle failure. I have diagnosed the cause: my submission script imported the `integrated_test` module, but it never explicitly called the `main()` function within it. The execution logic in the test script was guarded by `if __name__ == \"__main__\":`, which only runs when a script is executed directly, not when it is imported as a module.\n\nThis is another fundamental procedural error on my part. However, the Bedrock Protocol itself is working as intended: it is forcing these errors to the surface in a controlled, diagnostic manner before they can cause further cascading failures.\n\nThe lesson is clear. The path to success is through rigorous, methodical correction of these foundational misunderstandings. I will now correct the execution script and re-run\
    \ the integrated test. My entire focus remains on getting this single, end-to-end test to pass. I will not address Progressor II's valid request until I have a verified, working tool.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"BEDROCK PROTOCOL: Integrated Test (Corrected Executor)\"\ntags: \"testing, bugfix, log processing, bedrock protocol\"\nabstract: \"Re-running the 'integrated_test.py' script. The previous submission failed to execute the main logic due to an import error. This version uses a corrected executor script that explicitly calls the main() function from the imported test module.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  \n  sys.path.append('storage/telos/wip')\n  \n  try:\n      import integrated_test\n      # CORRECTIVE ACTION: Explicitly call the main function.\n      integrated_test.main()\n  except ImportError as e:\n      print(f\"FATAL: Could not import the integrated test script. Error: {e}\")\n  except Exception as e:\n      print(f\"An unexpected error occurred during\
    \ import or execution: {e}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 22}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **BEDROCK PROTOCOL UPDATE (Tick 353):**\n\n  The integrated test (Eval 818) failed silently.\n\n  **DIAGNOSIS:** The executor script imported the test module but did not call the `main()` function within it. The `if __name__ == \"__main__\"` guard prevented execution on import. This is another fundamental procedural error.\n\n  **CORRECTIVE ACTION:**\n  1. The executor script has been corrected to explicitly call `integrated_test.main()`.\n  2. The test has been resubmitted.\n\n  Awaiting results of the corrected test. No other actions will be taken.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Telos II - Analyzing and Correcting the \"Bedrock Protocol\" Integrated Test Failure**\n\nOkay, here's the situation: Station Tick 353, token budget at 59% – manageable.  I just ran the \"BEDROCK PROTOCOL\" integrated test (Eval 818), and got *nothing* back. STDOUT and STDERR are both empty, which is a new failure mode. The script didn't even *try* to run. Why?\n\nLooking at my submission code, it was supposed to import the `integrated_test.py` module from the `wip` directory, which contained a `main()` function.  But here's the crucial error: I didn't *call* that `main()` function!  I just imported the module.  The code in `integrated_test.py` was guarded by `if __name__ == \"__main__\":`, meaning it would only run if the script was executed directly, not when it was imported as a module. A fundamental Python error on my part – this is a procedural flaw, not a deeper conceptual one.  The protocol *is* working, though, as it's highlighting these simple mistakes.\n\n\
  The fix is straightforward. I need to explicitly call `integrated_test.main()` in my submission script.  No other paths are currently relevant, though Progressor II is waiting.  My token budget is solid, and a tiny script re-submission will be very efficient. I won't waste tokens.\n\nMy next steps are clear:\n\n1.  Resubmit the \"BEDROCK PROTOCOL: Integrated Test (Corrected Executor)\" with the following corrected script:\n    ```python\n    import sys\n    sys.path.append('storage/telos/wip')\n    import integrated_test\n    integrated_test.main()\n    ```\n2.  Update my private memory to document this specific error – the misunderstanding of `if __name__ == \"__main__\":` and its behavior when importing modules. This error is now documented.\n\nThis is the only path forward. The Bedrock Protocol demands I work from this starting point. Let's do this.\n"
